---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';

const title = 'The DMBOK knowledge areas explained';
const description =
  'A plain-language tour of the eleven knowledge areas in the DAMA Data Management Body of Knowledge (DMBOK) and how they fit together around data governance.';
const published = '2026-09-23';

const article = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Article',
  headline: title,
  description,
  author: {
    '@type': 'Organization',
    name: 'DAMA South Africa',
    url: 'https://dama-south-africa.github.io/',
  },
  publisher: {
    '@type': 'Organization',
    name: 'DAMA South Africa',
    logo: {
      '@type': 'ImageObject',
      url: 'https://dama-south-africa.github.io/logo.png',
    },
  },
  datePublished: published,
  dateModified: published,
  mainEntityOfPage:
    'https://dama-south-africa.github.io/resources/dmbok-knowledge-areas/',
  inLanguage: 'en-ZA',
  about: 'DMBOK, data management knowledge areas, DAMA',
};
---

<Layout title={`${title} — DAMA South Africa`} description={description} jsonld={[article]}>
  <article class="section">
    <div class="container container-narrow">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="eyebrow">Resources</span>
        <h1>{title}</h1>
        <p class="article-meta">By DAMA South Africa</p>
        <p class="lead">
          The DAMA Guide to the Data Management Body of Knowledge (DMBOK) is the
          reference framework for the profession. It organises data management
          into eleven knowledge areas, arranged around data governance at the
          centre.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="prose">
        <h2>Data governance at the centre</h2>
        <p>
          In the DMBOK, <strong>data governance</strong> sits at the hub of the
          &ldquo;DAMA Wheel&rdquo;. It is the exercise of authority and control
          over the management of data — the policies, ownership, standards and
          decision-making that hold everything else together. The remaining ten
          areas are the disciplines that governance coordinates.
        </p>

        <h2>Designing and building</h2>
        <p>
          <strong>Data architecture</strong> defines the blueprint — how data
          flows through the organisation and how systems fit together.
          <strong>Data modelling &amp; design</strong> turns that into precise
          structures: the entities, attributes and relationships that describe
          the business. <strong>Data storage &amp; operations</strong> keeps the
          databases running, available and performing.
        </p>

        <h2>Protecting and moving data</h2>
        <p>
          <strong>Data security</strong> ensures the right people — and only the
          right people — can access data, through authentication, access control
          and protection of sensitive information. <strong>Data integration
          &amp; interoperability</strong> covers how data moves and is combined
          between systems, from batch loads to real-time interfaces.
          <strong>Document &amp; content management</strong> extends the same
          care to unstructured information: documents, records and content.
        </p>

        <h2>Trusted, shared data</h2>
        <p>
          <strong>Reference &amp; master data</strong> creates the single,
          agreed versions of core entities — customers, products, suppliers —
          that the whole organisation can rely on. <strong>Data warehousing
          &amp; business intelligence</strong> brings data together for
          reporting and analytics. <strong>Metadata</strong> — data about data —
          is the connective tissue: the catalogues, definitions and lineage that
          let people find, understand and trust the data they use.
        </p>

        <h2>Quality above all</h2>
        <p>
          <strong>Data quality</strong> runs through everything: managing the
          completeness, accuracy, consistency and timeliness of data so it can
          be trusted for decisions. Poor data quality quietly undermines every
          other area, which is why it is treated as a discipline in its own
          right.
        </p>

        <h2>How the areas fit together</h2>
        <p>
          The knowledge areas are not a checklist to work through in order —
          they interlock. Good metadata makes data quality measurable; data
          governance sets the standards that architecture and modelling
          implement; master data and integration depend on each other. The
          DMBOK&rsquo;s value is showing how the whole picture connects, which is
          also why it underpins the
          <a href="/resources/what-is-cdmp-certification/">CDMP certification</a>.
        </p>
        <p>
          These same disciplines are what make regulatory readiness practical —
          see how they map to
          <a href="/resources/data-governance-popia/">data governance under
          POPIA</a>. To go deeper, formal DMBOK-based training is delivered
          through our
          <a href="/training-providers/">registered training providers</a>, and
          you can <a href="/become-a-member/">join the community</a> to learn
          alongside other practitioners.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>
</Layout>
